-- title:
--   Stationary quantum system: $\ast$-algebra, time evolution, invariant state
-- statement:
--   A **stationary quantum system** consists of
--
--   1. a unital $\ast$-algebra $\mathcal A$ over $\mathbb C$ (the observables);
--   2. a one-parameter group $(\tau_t)_{t\in\mathbb R}$ of $\ast$-automorphisms of $\mathcal A$, $\tau_0=\mathrm{id}$, $\tau_{s+t}=\tau_s\circ\tau_t$; we write $x(t)=\tau_t(x)$ (Heisenberg picture);
--   3. a state $\rho_0$ on $\mathcal A$ that is invariant, $\rho_0(\tau_t x)=\rho_0(x)$ for all $t$ and $x$.
--
--   This is the uncoupled detector of Sec. IV.A.2: "some physical system (described by some unspecified Hamiltonian $\hat H_{\rm det}$ and some unspecified density matrix $\hat\rho_0$) which is time-independent in the absence of coupling to the signal source", with $x(t)=e^{i\hat H_{\rm det}t/\hbar}\,x\,e^{-i\hat H_{\rm det}t/\hbar}$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Any one-parameter group of $\ast$-automorphisms is allowed, not only one generated by a Hamiltonian; "time-independent" is the invariance of $\rho_0$. No continuity of $\tau$ is assumed here; the regularity used is placed on the correlators of a `Detector`.
-- source:
--   Clerk, Devoret, Girvin, Marquardt, Schoelkopf, Introduction to Quantum Noise, Measurement and Amplification, arXiv:0810.4729v2, Sec. IV.A.2, p.21

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_QuantumNoise_IsState

namespace QuantumNoise

/-- A **stationary quantum system**: a unital ∗-algebra `𝒜` of observables over `ℂ`, a time
evolution `τ : ℝ → Aut⋆(𝒜)` that is a one-parameter group (`τ 0 = id`,
`τ (s + t) = τ s ∘ τ t`), and a state `ρ₀` invariant under it (`ρ₀ ∘ τ t = ρ₀`).

`τ t x` is the Heisenberg-picture operator `x(t)`. This is the uncoupled detector of Clerk et
al., arXiv:0810.4729v2, Sec. IV.A.2, p.21 (printed p.21, PDF p.21): "some physical system
(described by some unspecified Hamiltonian Ĥdet and some unspecified density matrix ρ̂₀) which
is time-independent in the absence of coupling to the signal source"; "the operators Î and F̂
are Heisenberg operators with respect to the detector Hamiltonian" (p.21, after (4.3)).

**Formalization Note** The Hamiltonian enters only through the Heisenberg evolution
`x(t) = e^{iĤt/ħ} x e^{−iĤt/ħ}`, which is a one-parameter group of ∗-automorphisms; any such
group is allowed (generalization). "Time-independent" is the invariance `ρ₀ (τ t x) = ρ₀ x`.
No continuity of `τ` is assumed here; the regularity actually used is put on the correlators in
`Detector`. -/
structure StationaryDynamics (𝒜 : Type*) [Ring 𝒜] [StarRing 𝒜] [Algebra ℂ 𝒜]
    [StarModule ℂ 𝒜] where
  /-- The time evolution `x ↦ x(t)`. -/
  τ : ℝ → (𝒜 ≃⋆ₐ[ℂ] 𝒜)
  τ_zero : ∀ x, τ 0 x = x
  τ_add : ∀ s t x, τ (s + t) x = τ s (τ t x)
  /-- The state `⟨·⟩₀` of the uncoupled system. -/
  ρ₀ : 𝒜 →ₗ[ℂ] ℂ
  isState : IsState ρ₀
  stationary : ∀ t x, ρ₀ (τ t x) = ρ₀ x

end QuantumNoise


